{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nThe user is asking me to implement a `solve(case)` function for a grammar-guided node reduction task. Let me analyze the requirements:\n\n1. The input is a tree of nodes with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n2. Required symbols must always remain represented in the retained nodes\n3. Never remove root, required symbols (nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols), or nodes with retained children\n4. Consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (process deeper nodes first)\n5. Use node ID as stable tiebreak at equal depth\n6. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\n7. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order\n\nLet me think through the algorithm:\n\n1. Build the tree structure from the nodes\n2. Process nodes in order: deeper nodes first, then by node ID as tiebreak\n3. For each node (candidate for removal):\n   - Skip if not removable\n   - Skip if it's the root\n   - Skip if its symbol is in required_symbols (wait, the required_symbols constraint might be about whether the symbol is still represented by other nodes)\n   - Skip if it has retained children\n   - Tentatively remove it and check if all required symbols still have at least one representative in retained nodes\n4. If removal is valid, mark node as removed\n5. Return retained node IDs (sorted) and removal order\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\"\n\nSo \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? But that seems to conflict with \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" which suggests we're checking coverage.\n\nActually I think \"never remove a node with a required symbol\" means nodes whose symbol field is in the required_symbols list. But wait, the \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" suggests we check if all required symbols are still covered by remaining nodes after removal.\n\nLet me re-read more carefully:\n\n- \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\" - this means:\n  1. Never remove root\n  2. Never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols (hard constraint)\n  3. Never remove a node that has retained children\n\n- \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this is an additional check\n\nActually, thinking about it differently:\n- \"Never remove... a required symbol\" could mean never remove a node whose symbol is a required symbol\n- But then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would be checking that after removing a non-required-symbol node, the required symbols are still covered by other nodes\n\nWait, if we never remove nodes with required symbols, then required symbols are always represented. So the tentative check would always pass trivially. Unless the intent is that required symbols can be represented by nodes that don't have `removable=false`.\n\nActually, re-reading the fixture: required_symbols are [\"root\", \"s1\"]. Looking at the nodes:\n- root has symbol \"root\"\n- n1 has symbol \"s1\" (removable=false)\n- n4 has symbol \"s1\" (removable=true)\n- n6 has symbol \"s0\" (removable=true)\n\nSo if we remove n4 (symbol s1, removable=true), s1 is still represented by n1. The check \"all required symbols remain represented\" would pass.\n\nSo the algorithm is:\n1. A node can be removed if:\n   - `removable == true`\n   - It's not the root\n   - After removal, all required symbols are still represented by at least one retained node\n   - It has no retained children\n\nWait, but \"Never remove... a required symbol\" - if n4 has symbol s1 which is a required symbol, can it be removed? The rule says \"never remove a required symbol\" which I'd interpret as never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But then the tentative check about required symbols being represented wouldn't matter...\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. Maybe \"a required symbol\" refers to the concept of a symbol that must be preserved, not a specific node. The rule \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" enforces that required_symbols stay covered.\n\nSo the interpretation would be:\n- Never remove root (the root node)\n- Never remove a node if doing so would leave a required symbol unrepresented (i.e., no other retained node has that symbol)\n- Never remove a node with retained children\n\nAnd separately: `removable` field must be true\n\nLet me look at the examples more carefully to infer the expected behavior.\n\nFor case fg1-se-pe-01-a-000:\nNodes:\n- root (depth 0, symbol \"root\", removable=false)\n- n1 (depth 1, symbol \"s1\", removable=false)\n- n2 (depth 1, symbol \"s2\", removable=true)\n- n3 (depth 1, symbol \"s0\", removable=false)\n- n4 (depth 2, symbol \"s1\", removable=true) - parent n2\n- n5 (depth 2, symbol \"s2\", removable=false) - parent n3\n- n6 (depth 2, symbol \"s0\", removable=true) - parent n1\n\nRequired symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order (deeper first, then by node ID):\nDepth 2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by ID: n4, n5, n6)\nDepth 1: n1, n2, n3 (sorted by ID)\nDepth 0: root\n\nFor n4 (depth 2, symbol \"s1\", removable=true, parent n2):\n- removable: yes\n- not root\n- After removing n4, symbol \"s1\" is still represented by n1 ✓\n- n4 has no children\n- So n4 can be removed\n\nFor n5 (depth 2, symbol \"s2\", removable=false):\n- removable: no → skip\n\nFor n6 (depth 2, symbol \"s0\", removable=true, parent n1):\n- removable: yes\n- not root\n- After removing n6, \"root\" still represented by root ✓, \"s1\" still represented by n1 ✓\n- n6 has no children\n- So n6 can be removed\n\nNow at depth 1:\nn1 (removable=false) → skip\nn2 (removable=true, parent root):\n- n4 was removed, so n2 has no retained children\n- After removing n2, \"s2\" is not required, \"root\" still represented, \"s1\" still represented\n- n2 can be removed\n\nn3 (removable=false) → skip\n\nroot (removable=false) → skip\n\nSo the removal order would be: n4, n6, n2\nRetained: root, n1, n3, n5 → sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\n\nWait, but the problem says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" so we process depth 2 first, then depth 1, then depth 0.\n\nAt depth 2, candidates sorted by ID: n4, n5, n6\n- n4: removable=true, no children → check if s1 still covered after removal → n1 still has s1 → yes → remove n4\n- n5: removable=false → skip\n- n6: removable=true, no children → check required symbols → all covered → remove n6\n\nAt depth 1, candidates sorted by ID: n1, n2, n3\n- n1: removable=false → skip\n- n2: removable=true, children=[n4] but n4 was removed, so no retained children → check required symbols → all covered → remove n2\n- n3: removable=false → skip\n\nAt depth 0: root → removable=false → skip\n\nRemoval order: [n4, n6, n2]\nRetained: {root, n1, n3, n5} → sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\n\nThat seems right based on the algorithm.\n\nNow let me also think about whether \"retained children\" means immediate children or all descendants. I'll assume immediate children for now.\n\nActually, \"retained children\" should mean immediate children that are still retained (not yet removed). When we're processing in order, a node's children at deeper levels would have already been processed.\n\nLet me code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build data structures\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retained set\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    \n    def get_represented_symbols(retained_set):\n        return set(node_map[nid][\"symbol\"] for nid in retained_set)\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by node ID\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if it has retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove: check if all required symbols still represented\n        tentative_retained = retained - {nid}\n        represented = get_represented_symbols(tentative_retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(represented):\n            continue\n        \n        # Remove the node\n        retained = tentative_retained\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained\": retained_sorted,\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but the root check: root has `removable=false`, so it'll be skipped by the `if not node[\"removable\"]` check. Good.\n\nFor required symbols: if a node has symbol \"s1\" and is removable=true, can it be removed if another node still has symbol \"s1\"? Yes, because after removal \"s1\" is still represented. The tentative check handles this.\n\nLet me trace through case 000 again:\n- candidates sorted by (-depth, id): n4(d2), n5(d2), n6(d2), n1(d1), n2(d1), n3(d1), root(d0)\n\nWait, sorting by (-depth, id): \n- depth 2: n4, n5, n6 (alphabetical: n4 \u003c n5 \u003c n6) ✓\n- depth 1: n1, n2, n3 ✓\n- depth 0: root ✓\n\nProcessing n4:\n- removable=true ✓\n- children of n4: none (no node has parent=\"n4\") ✓\n- tentative_retained = all - {n4}\n- represented symbols: {root, s1, s2, s0, s2} = {root, s1, s2, s0}\n- required: {root, s1} ⊆ {root, s1, s2, s0} ✓\n- Remove n4, removal_order = [n4]\n\nProcessing n5:\n- removable=false → skip\n\nProcessing n6:\n- removable=true ✓\n- children of n6: none ✓\n- tentative_retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5} - {n6}... wait, n4 was already removed\n- retained after n4 removed: {root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6}\n- tentative: {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}\n- symbols: {root, s1, s2, s0, s2} = {root, s1, s2, s0}\n- required: {root, s1} ✓\n- Remove n6, removal_order = [n4, n6]\n\nProcessing n1:\n- removable=false → skip\n\nProcessing n2:\n- removable=true ✓\n- children of n2: [n4] - is n4 retained? No (was removed)\n- So no retained children ✓\n- retained currently: {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}\n- tentative: {root, n1, n3, n5}\n- symbols: {root, s1, s0, s2} \n- required: {root, s1} ✓\n- Remove n2, removal_order = [n4, n6, n2]\n\nProcessing n3:\n- removable=false → skip\n\nProcessing root:\n- removable=false → skip\n\nFinal retained: {root, n1, n3, n5} → sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\nremoval_order: [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"]\n\nThis makes sense! Let me also verify the \"stable tie-breaking\" - node IDs sorted alphabetically. \"n4\" \u003c \"n5\" \u003c \"n6\" alphabetically? \"n4\" vs \"n5\": \"4\" \u003c \"5\" ✓. But wait, for IDs like \"n10\" and \"n9\", alphabetical would give \"n10\" \u003c \"n9\" which might not be intended. However looking at the fixture, IDs are simple like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. I'll use standard string sorting which should work for these cases.\n\nLet me also add validation as the problem mentions \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\"\n\nThe required fields to validate:\n- Each node must have: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n- payload must have: nodes, required_symbols\n\nLet me write the complete implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate required fields\n    if \"payload\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing 'payload' in case\")\n    \n    payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    \n    if \"nodes\" not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing 'nodes' in payload\")\n    if \"required_symbols\" not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing 'required_symbols' in payload\")\n    \n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate each node\n    required_node_fields = {\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"}\n    for node in nodes:\n        missing = required_node_fields - set(node.keys())\n        if missing:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node missing fields: {missing}\")\n    \n    # Build data structures\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retained set\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    \n    def get_represented_symbols(retained_set):\n        return set(node_map[nid][\"symbol\"] for nid in retained_set)\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by node ID (stable tiebreak)\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if it has retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove: check if all required symbols still represented\n        tentative_retained = retained - {nid}\n        represented = get_represented_symbols(tentative_retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(represented):\n            continue\n        \n        # Remove the node\n        retained = tentative_retained\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained\": retained_sorted,\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks correct to me. The response format must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string.\n\nLet me finalize the JSON encoding. I need to be careful with newlines (\\n), backslashes, and quotes.\n\nThe response should be:\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nLet me write the complete JSON response. The instructions say not to use markdown code fences and return exactly one JSON object.\n\nOne thing to double-check: the `sorted()` on the retained set - Python's default sort for strings is lexicographic. For IDs like \"n1\", \"n2\", ..., \"n10\", lexicographic would give \"n1\", \"n10\", \"n2\", etc. But looking at the fixtures, we have at most n6, so this shouldn't be an issue. The problem says \"sorted retained IDs\" without specifying numeric or lexicographic order. I'll use lexicographic (default Python sort) which matches string comparison.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - this could mean sorted in a natural/numeric way. But since the IDs in the fixture are all single-digit (n1-n6 plus \"root\"), lexicographic and numeric would agree. I'll stick with default sort.\n\nNow let me format the final response as a proper JSON string.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required top-level fields\\n    if \\\"payload\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'payload' in case\\\")\\n    payload = case[\\\"payload\\\"]\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' in payload\\\")\\n    if \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'required_symbols' in payload\\\")\\n\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    # Validate each node has required fields\\n    required_node_fields = {\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        missing = required_node_fields - set(node.keys())\\n        if missing:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {node.get('id', '?')} missing fields: {missing}\\\")\\n\\n    # Build lookup structures\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    # Map each node to its immediate children\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None:\\n            if n[\\\"parent\\\"] not in children:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {n['id']} references unknown parent {n['parent']}\\\")\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    # Start with all nodes retained\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n\\n    def represented_symbols(retained_set):\\n        return set(node_map[nid][\\\"symbol\\\"] for nid in retained_set)\\n\\n    # Process candidates: deeper nodes first, then lexicographic node ID as stable tiebreak\\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    for node in candidates:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: must be marked removable\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: no retained children (leaf-first removal)\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: all required symbols must remain represented after removal\\n        tentative = retained - {nid}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(represented_symbols(tentative)):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Commit removal\\n        retained = tentative\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained\\\": sorted(retained),\\n        \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01WHvFC0SMZ37yo7HSZqM8ws","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2089},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2089,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":26,"output_tokens":4924,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4248},"service_tier":"standard"}}